    "cons": [
      "Not for most Bradford White heaters with an outlet-nipple anode",
      "Magnesium can feed rotten-egg smell in some well water",
      "Socket not included"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right replacement rod for most standard residential tanks with a dedicated hex anode port and a normal water supply.",
    "description": "For most homeowners replacing a worn rod, this is the one to buy. Magnesium is the material most residential tanks ship with and the most electrochemically active of the standard choices, which means it gives the strongest protection to the steel tank in ordinary city water. Ozek makes the rod 44 inches long and segmented, so it can be fed into the tank a section at a time where a solid rod would hit the ceiling, a joist or the ductwork above the heater. The connection is a standard 3/4-inch NPT hex head for a dedicated anode port, and the kit includes a roll of pipe sealing tape. What earns it the top spot is that the listing tells you clearly where it does not fit: most Bradford White heaters that use an outlet-nipple anode, models without a dedicated 3/4-inch NPT anode port, and commercial heaters unless they use a standard hex anode. That is the most common mistake in this category, and Ozek tells you how to avoid it before you order. The trade-off is chemistry. In some well water, magnesium reacts with sulfate-reducing bacteria and produces a rotten-egg smell in the hot water. If your hot water already smells, skip magnesium and buy the Ozek aluminum-zinc version or a powered anode instead. You will also need a 1-1/16-inch socket."
  },
  {
    "id": "corro-protec-40-89-powered",
    "name": "Corro-Protec 40-89 Gal Powered Anode Rod with Installation Kit",
    "category": "Powered Titanium Anode, 40-89 Gal Tanks, Dedicated Anode Port, 20-Year Warranty",
