      "Designed for any hardness, including softened water",
      "20-year warranty, manufactured in Canada since 2006",
      "Kit includes sockets and PTFE tape"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs a power outlet near the heater",
      "No protection while it is unplugged",
      "Not for Bradford White heaters (a separate outlet model exists)"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The rod to buy if you want to stop replacing anodes, live with softened water, or need to cure a rotten-egg smell for good.",
    "description": "A powered anode replaces the sacrificial metal rod with a titanium electrode fed by a small current from a wall adapter, and Corro-Protec is the best-known name in the category. This 40 to 89 gallon model fits the dedicated anode port on top of the heater and, per the listing, suits every brand except Bradford White, which has its own outlet-mounted version. The kit includes the sockets and PTFE tape. The reason to choose it is permanence. A magnesium or aluminum rod dissolves by design and needs checking every few years; a powered anode does not wear away, and Corro-Protec backs it with a 20-year warranty and says it has been manufactured in Canada since 2006. The listing claims it eliminates sulfur smell in hot water within 24 hours, defends against any level of hardness including the higher corrosiveness of softened water, and reduces limescale. That makes it the obvious choice for homes with a water softener, where sacrificial rods disappear quickly, and for smelly hot water on a well. The real trade-off is dependence on power. It needs an outlet within reach of its cord, and while it is unplugged the tank has no anode protection at all, so a tripped breaker or an unplugged adapter matters. If there is no outlet nearby, an aluminum-zinc rod is the simpler fix for odor."
  },
  {
    "id": "corro-protec-bradford-white-outlet",
    "name": "Corro-Protec Powered Anode Rod for Bradford White & Hot Water Outlet",
    "category": "Powered Titanium Anode, Hot-Water-Outlet Install, 10-120 Gal Tanks, 20-Year Warranty",
    "badge": "Best for Bradford White",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Installs in the hot water outlet, where Bradford White fits its anode",
      "Covers 10- to 120-gallon heaters, per the listing",
      "12-foot power cord and step-by-step instructions",
      "Title states it is WQA-certified",
      "20-year warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs a nearby outlet",
      "Outlet installation means disconnecting the hot water line",
      "Unprotected tank if the adapter is unplugged"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The powered anode for Bradford White heaters and any tank whose anode lives in the hot water outlet.",
    "description": "Bradford White heaters do not have a separate hex anode port on most residential models. The anode is built into the hot water outlet nipple, which is why ordinary hex-head rods do not fit them and why so many owners order the wrong part. This Corro-Protec model is designed for that layout. The listing describes installation in the hot water outlet instead of a dedicated port, says it is the standard version for Bradford White, and covers tanks from 10 to 120 gallons. It ships with step-by-step instructions and a 12-foot power cord, which is generous and helps when the nearest outlet is not right beside the heater. As with the hex-port model, the titanium electrode does not wear away, the listing claims it eliminates sulfur smell in hot water within 24 hours and protects in softened water, and it carries a 20-year warranty. The product title states it is WQA-certified. The trade-off is installation work: fitting an anode in the outlet means disconnecting the hot water line from the top of the heater, which is more involved than swapping a hex rod, and the tank has no protection whenever the adapter is unplugged. If you want a sacrificial rod for an outlet-style heater instead, the Water Connection aluminum-zinc outlet anode is the matching non-powered option."
  },
  {
    "id": "ozek-44-segmented-aluminum-zinc",
    "name": "Ozek 44 in Segmented Aluminum Zinc Anode Rod (3/4 in NPT)",
    "category": "Aluminum-Zinc, Flexible 44-in, Stainless Steel Joints, 3/4-in NPT Hex",
