    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Aluminum-zinc alloy, the standard fix for smelly hot water",
      "44-inch flexible design with stainless steel joints",
      "3/4-inch NPT thread fits most dedicated anode ports",
      "Listed for AO Smith, GE, Kenmore, State, Whirlpool, Rheem and more",
      "PTFE tape included"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Less active than magnesium in very soft water",
      "Not for outlet-nipple heaters such as most Bradford White",
      "Does not cure bacteria in the well itself"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The simplest non-powered fix when hot water smells of rotten eggs and cold water does not.",
    "description": "If your hot water smells of rotten eggs and your cold water does not, the heater's magnesium rod is the usual suspect. Sulfate-reducing bacteria in some water supplies react with the magnesium and produce hydrogen sulfide in the tank. Swapping to aluminum-zinc is the standard plumber's fix, because the zinc content suppresses that reaction while the rod still protects the tank. This Ozek rod is the aluminum-zinc twin of our top pick, with the same 44-inch segmented length and stainless steel joints that let it squeeze into a heater with little headroom. It has a standard 3/4-inch NPT connection and is listed as compatible with heaters from AO Smith, Sears, GE, Kenmore, State, Whirlpool, Reliance, Rheem, Richmond and other brands, with PTFE tape in the set. The listing also notes a fit exception, which in practice means outlet-anode tanks such as most Bradford White models. The trade-off is protection strength. Aluminum-zinc is less active than magnesium, so in very soft water it protects the tank a little less vigorously; in hard water that difference matters less. It is also not a cure for bacteria in the well; if the cold water smells too, the problem is upstream. For smell plus permanent protection, or with a water softener, step up to the Corro-Protec powered anode."
  },
  {
    "id": "water-connection-alzn-outlet-bradford-white",
    "name": "Water Connection Aluminum Zinc Outlet Anode Rod for Bradford White (3/4 in NPT x 5 in Nipple x 48 in)",
    "category": "Aluminum-Zinc Combo/Outlet Anode, 3/4-in NPT x 5-in Nipple, 48-in, 3/4-in Diameter",
    "badge": "Best Combo Outlet Anode",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Installs in the hot water outlet, the usual Bradford White layout",
      "A420 aluminum-zinc alloy to Mil-A-18001K/W, per the listing",
      "Thick 3/4-inch diameter gives more metal to sacrifice",
      "48-inch length for full-size tanks",
      "Lifetime warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Solid 48-inch rod needs plenty of overhead clearance",
      "Outlet install means disconnecting the hot water line",
      "Wrong part for heaters with a dedicated hex anode port"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The sacrificial replacement for Bradford White and other outlet-anode tanks, with an odor-fighting alloy and a thick core.",
    "description": "A combo or outlet anode is a rod built onto a threaded nipple that screws into the heater's hot water outlet, with the hot water line connecting to the top of it. Bradford White uses this layout on most residential tanks, and a few A.O. Smith models do too, according to this listing. If your heater has no separate hex plug on top, this is the type of rod you need. The Water Connection rod has a 3/4-inch NPT outlet head on a 5-inch nipple and a 48-inch rod, and the listing says 3-inch and other variants are available, so measure the nipple on your existing rod before ordering. The alloy is listed as A420 aluminum-zinc meeting U.S. Mil-A-18001K/W, chosen to prevent the rotten-egg smell that magnesium can cause, and the rod is a solid 3/4-inch diameter, which the listing points out gives more metal to sacrifice than the 5/8-inch rods some competitors use. A lifetime warranty is included. The trade-off is installation. A solid 48-inch rod needs about that much clearance above the heater, and fitting it means disconnecting the hot water line. In a low basement, the Eastman flexible outlet rod is easier; for no future replacements at all, the Corro-Protec outlet model is the powered alternative."
  },
  {
    "id": "water-connection-solid-magnesium-hex-44",
    "name": "Water Connection Magnesium Anode Rod, Hex Head 3/4 NPT x 44-in Solid",
    "category": "Solid Magnesium, Hex Head, 3/4-in NPT, 44-in",
    "badge": "Best Solid Rod",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "One-piece solid magnesium rod, the same style most tanks ship with",
      "Standard hex head with 3/4-inch NPT thread",
      "44-inch length suits most 40- and 50-gallon tanks",
      "No hinge joints to wear or separate",
      "Listed for Rheem, AO Smith, Reliance, Richmond and more"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs about 44 inches of clearance above the tank",
      "Magnesium can cause odor with some well water",
      "The listing gives little detail beyond the title"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A straightforward like-for-like magnesium replacement when you have the headroom to drop in a one-piece rod.",
    "description": "If there is open space above your water heater, a solid one-piece rod is the closest match to what the factory installed. This Water Connection rod is magnesium with a standard hex head and a 3/4-inch NPT thread, 44 inches long, and the listing names fit with Rheem, AO Smith, Reliance, Richmond and other brands. A one-piece rod has no hinge joints, so there is nothing to separate or corrode at a link, and the full length of metal sits evenly in the tank. That is the main reason some plumbers prefer solid rods wherever they fit. Length matters for coverage: a 44-inch rod suits most 40- and 50-gallon tall tanks, but measure the old one, because a rod far shorter than the tank leaves the bottom less protected. The trade-off is clearance. You need roughly the rod's length of free space above the anode port, or you will be bending the rod or tipping the heater, and in many basements and closets that space does not exist. In that case buy a segmented rod such as the Ozek magnesium. The other trade-off is that this listing gives very little detail beyond its title, so check the thread and hex size on your heater before ordering. Like any magnesium rod, it is the wrong choice where hot water already smells."
  },
  {
    "id": "eastman-60286-flexible-magnesium-outlet",
    "name": "Eastman 42 Inch Flexible Magnesium Anode Rod, 3/4 x 3 in NPT Stainless Steel Nipple (60286)",
    "category": "Flexible Magnesium Outlet Anode, 3/4 x 3-in Stainless Nipple, 42-in, 4 Links",
    "badge": "Best Flexible Outlet Anode",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Mounts on a 3/4 x 3-inch NPT stainless steel nipple",
