        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is magnesium or aluminum better for an anode rod?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Magnesium protects more actively and suits soft or ordinary city water, but it can cause rotten-egg smell in some well water. Aluminum-zinc lasts well in hard water and is the standard fix for smelly hot water. If you have a water softener or persistent odor, a powered titanium anode avoids the choice altogether."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do powered anode rods really work?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Powered anodes use a titanium electrode and a small current instead of dissolving metal, and they are widely used to stop rotten-egg smell and protect tanks in softened water. Corro-Protec backs its rods with a 20-year warranty. They only protect while plugged in, so they need a reliable outlet and a quick check now and then."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size anode rod do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most residential tank heaters take a 3/4-inch NPT anode about 40 to 44 inches long, removed with a 1-1/16-inch socket. Match the length of the old rod, use a shorter rod for short tanks, and avoid RV rods, which are only a few inches long. Check whether your heater has a hex port or an outlet anode first."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I know if my water heater has a combo anode?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Look at the top of the tank. If there is a separate hex plug, you have a dedicated anode port. If there is no hex plug and the hot water pipe connects through a fitting on a short nipple, the anode is built into the outlet. Bradford White uses outlet anodes on most residential models."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a flexible anode rod?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Flexible segmented rods protect the tank in the same way as solid ones and are designed for heaters with little headroom. They feed in a section at a time, and Eastman lists a 12-inch clearance minimum for its outlet rod. Solid rods have no joints, so they are a good choice where you have the full clearance."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should a water heater anode rod be replaced?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many manufacturers suggest inspecting every two to three years and replacing when the core wire is exposed or most of the metal is gone. Softened water, hot settings and heavy use wear rods faster, so check more often in those homes. A powered anode does not need replacing but should be checked to confirm it is powered."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
