        <div id="materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Which Anode Rod Material? Magnesium for Protection, Aluminum-Zinc for Smell
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy magnesium for the strongest protection in ordinary water, aluminum-zinc if your hot water smells of rotten eggs, and a powered anode if you want to stop replacing rods or you run a water softener. Material is the first decision, because it decides whether the new rod fixes your problem or causes one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Magnesium</h3>

          <p>
            Magnesium is the most active of the common sacrificial metals, so it gives the tank the most protection, especially in soft or low-mineral water. Many residential tanks ship with it. It wears faster than aluminum and, in some well water, feeds sulfate-reducing bacteria that turn the hot water smelly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Aluminum-Zinc</h3>

          <p>
            Aluminum alloyed with a small amount of zinc is the standard choice for odor problems, because the zinc suppresses the hydrogen sulfide reaction. It lasts well in hard water. It protects a little less actively than magnesium in very soft water, but for most homes with smelly hot water it is the right swap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plain Aluminum</h3>

          <p>
            Plain aluminum rods are common as factory anodes in hard water regions because they last longer there. As a replacement, aluminum-zinc does everything plain aluminum does and helps with odor too, so we did not include a plain aluminum pick.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Powered Titanium</h3>

          <p>
            A powered anode uses a titanium electrode and a small current from a wall adapter instead of a metal that dissolves. It does not wear away, and the leading models carry long warranties. It needs an outlet, and it protects nothing while it is unplugged.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hex-vs-outlet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Hex Head or Combo Outlet? Check the Port Before You Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Look at the top of your heater before ordering anything. If there is a separate hex plug, you need a hex-head rod. If there is no hex plug and the anode is built into the hot water outlet, you need a combo or outlet anode. Ordering the wrong style is the most common anode-buying mistake.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dedicated Hex Anode Port</h3>

          <p>
            Most Rheem, A.O. Smith, State, GE, Reliance and Richmond tanks have a dedicated anode port: a hex head, usually 1-1/16 inch, sitting on its own fitting on the tank top, sometimes under a cap or insulation. Hex-head rods with a 3/4-inch NPT thread fit these.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Combo or Outlet Anode</h3>

          <p>
            A combo anode is a rod attached to a threaded nipple that screws into the hot water outlet, with the hot water pipe connecting on top. Bradford White uses this layout on most residential models. To replace it you disconnect the hot water line, so the job is a little bigger.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two Anodes in One Tank</h3>

          <p>
            Some larger or longer-warranty tanks carry both a hex anode and an outlet anode. If yours does, check and replace both. Our how-to on <a href="/water-heater-anode-rod-replacement/">water heater anode rod replacement</a> shows how to find each one and what a worn rod looks like.
          </p>

