        <div id="size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Size Anode Rod Do I Need? 3/4-Inch NPT and the Right Length
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most residential tank heaters take a 3/4-inch NPT anode about 40 to 44 inches long, removed with a 1-1/16-inch socket. Confirm all three on your own heater, because RV heaters, short tanks and commercial units differ.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thread</h3>

          <p>
            Standard residential hex anodes use a 3/4-inch NPT thread. RV water heaters commonly use 1/2-inch or 3/4-inch rods only a few inches long, which is why so many RV rods appear in search results. They are the wrong part for a house tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Length</h3>

          <p>
            Match the old rod's length or the tank's recommendation. A rod that is much shorter than the tank leaves the lower part of the tank with less protection. Short and lowboy tanks take shorter rods; flexible rods can usually be shortened at a joint if needed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Diameter</h3>

          <p>
            Thicker rods carry more metal and last longer. Many replacements use 5/8-inch links; some solid rods are 3/4 inch. In softened or aggressive water, more metal means more time between checks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Socket Size</h3>

          <p>
            A 1-1/16-inch socket is standard for hex anodes. Some kits include one. Factory rods are often very tight, so an impact wrench or a long breaker bar is commonly needed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flexible-vs-solid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Flexible vs Solid Anode Rods: Ceiling Height Decides
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy a solid rod if you have roughly the rod's length of clear space above the heater; buy a flexible segmented rod if you do not. Protection is similar; installation is not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solid Rods</h3>

          <p>
            A one-piece rod is what most tanks ship with. It has no joints to wear and drops straight in. The catch is that a 44-inch rod needs about 44 inches of clearance above the port, and many heaters sit under joists, ducts or shelves.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flexible Segmented Rods</h3>

          <p>
            Segmented rods are short metal links joined by a cable or stainless hinges, so they can be fed in one section at a time with only a foot or so of headroom. Eastman lists a 12-inch minimum for its outlet rod. This is the practical answer for basements, closets and attics.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Measure Before You Order</h3>

          <p>
            Measure from the top of the anode port to the lowest obstruction above it. If the number is less than the rod length, go flexible. If the old rod will not come out in one piece, it can usually be bent as it is withdrawn.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="powered-anodes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Are Powered Anode Rods Worth It? Yes, for Softened or Smelly Water
          </h2>

          <p>
            A powered anode is worth it if you have a water softener, smelly hot water, or simply do not want to check and replace a sacrificial rod every few years. For a standard tank on ordinary city water, a magnesium rod does the job for less.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How They Work</h3>

          <p>
            Instead of a metal that dissolves to protect the steel, a powered anode uses a titanium electrode that carries a small, regulated current from a wall adapter. The current does the protecting, so the electrode itself does not wear away.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where They Shine</h3>

          <p>
            Softened water is more conductive and consumes sacrificial rods fast; a powered anode is not consumed. The current also stops the hydrogen sulfide reaction that causes rotten-egg smell, which is why Corro-Protec lists odor removal within 24 hours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Trade-Offs</h3>

          <p>
            You need an outlet within reach of the cord, and the tank has no anode protection while the adapter is unplugged or the circuit is off. Check that the indicator light, where fitted, stays on. Some manufacturers have views on non-factory anodes, so read your heater's warranty terms first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rotten-egg-smell" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Anode Rod Fixes Rotten-Egg Smell? Aluminum-Zinc or Powered
          </h2>

          <p>
            If only the hot water smells, an aluminum-zinc or powered anode usually fixes it. If the cold water smells too, the anode is not the main problem, and a new rod will not cure it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Heater Smells</h3>

          <p>
            Sulfate-reducing bacteria in some water supplies react with hydrogen produced at a magnesium anode and give off hydrogen sulfide. The warm tank is an ideal place for it to build up, which is why the smell shows up at hot taps first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choosing the Rod</h3>

          <p>
            Aluminum-zinc is the low-cost fix and needs no power. A powered anode removes the cause entirely and suits homes that also have a softener. Disinfecting the tank when you swap the rod helps clear existing bacteria.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When It Is Not the Anode</h3>

          <p>
            Smelly cold water points to the well, the pipes or the supply. Our guide <a href="/why-does-my-water-smell-like-rotten-eggs/">why does my water smell like rotten eggs</a> walks through finding the source in a few minutes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="softened-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Softened Water Eats Anode Rods Faster
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you run a salt-based softener, expect a sacrificial anode to wear out noticeably faster, and check it more often or switch to a powered anode.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Softeners Speed It Up</h3>

          <p>
            Ion exchange swaps calcium and magnesium for sodium, which makes water more conductive. The more conductive the water, the faster the galvanic reaction consumes the anode. Corro-Protec states its rods are designed for the increased corrosiveness of softened water for exactly this reason.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to Do About It</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the softener: it protects the rest of the house. If you are choosing one, our <a href="/best-water-softener-system/">best water softener systems</a> guide covers sizing and settings, since over-softening for no reason adds nothing. Then either fit a powered anode or plan on inspecting a sacrificial rod every year or two rather than every few years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="old-heater" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is a New Anode Worth It on an Old Water Heater?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Usually yes on a heater under about eight years old with a dry, sound tank; usually no on one that is already rusting or leaking. An anode protects a tank that is still intact; it cannot repair one that is not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Find the Age First</h3>

          <p>
            The serial number on the data plate encodes the manufacture date. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-tell-the-age-of-a-water-heater/">how to tell the age of a water heater</a> decodes it for the major brands.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Tank</h3>

          <p>
            Rusty hot water, weeping at the base or a damp pan are signs the tank lining has already failed. At that point, a new heater is the better spend. Compare options in our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heaters</a> and <a href="/best-electric-water-heater/">best electric water heaters</a> guides.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan the Next One</h3>

          <p>
            When you do buy a new heater, note the install date and check the anode in the first few years, especially with softened water. It is the cheapest way to get the full life out of the tank.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Warranty, Lead-Free and Certification Claims: Read the Listing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Anode listings make big claims. Trust the ones that are specific, such as thread size, length, alloy and warranty terms, and treat vague performance promises with caution.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Your Heater's Warranty</h3>

          <p>
            Some heater manufacturers specify which anode type may be used. Check the manual before switching material or fitting a powered anode. Corro-Protec states its rods are compatible with your heater warranty under U.S. Magnuson-Moss law.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Certifications</h3>

          <p>
            Look for certifications named in the listing, such as WQA certification or NSF/ANSI 372 for lead content. The Corro-Protec Bradford White listing states WQA certification in its title; for the others, ask the seller if it matters to you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Beware Mislabelled Listings</h3>

          <p>
            Some cheap rods are described as both magnesium and powered in the same listing, which is impossible. Skip any listing that contradicts itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering a Hex Rod for a Bradford White Heater</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most Bradford White residential tanks put the anode in the hot water outlet, not a separate hex port. A standard hex-head rod will not fit. Look at the tank top first and buy a combo or outlet anode if there is no hex plug.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an RV Anode for a House Tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Search results are full of short 1/2-inch and 9-inch RV rods. A residential tank needs a 3/4-inch NPT rod roughly the length of the old one, usually 40 to 44 inches.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fitting Magnesium When the Hot Water Already Smells</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Magnesium is often the cause of rotten-egg smell in some well water. Replacing a smelly magnesium rod with another magnesium rod brings the smell straight back. Choose aluminum-zinc or powered.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Solid Rod for a Low Ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A solid 44-inch rod needs about 44 inches of clearance. Without it, you end up tipping or draining the heater. Measure first and buy a flexible segmented rod if space is short.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Softener Shortens Anode Life</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Softened water consumes sacrificial anodes faster. Homes with a softener should check the rod more often or switch to a powered anode rather than waiting years.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

