        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best water heater anode rod?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most standard tanks with a dedicated hex port, the Ozek 44 in Segmented Magnesium Anode Rod is the best choice: magnesium protection, a 3/4-inch NPT thread and a flexible design for low ceilings. Choose aluminum-zinc for rotten-egg smell, a Corro-Protec powered anode for softened water, and an outlet anode for Bradford White heaters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is magnesium or aluminum better for an anode rod?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Magnesium protects more actively and suits soft or ordinary city water, but it can cause rotten-egg smell in some well water. Aluminum-zinc lasts well in hard water and is the standard fix for smelly hot water. If you have a water softener or persistent odor, a powered titanium anode avoids the choice altogether.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do powered anode rods really work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Powered anodes use a titanium electrode and a small current instead of dissolving metal, and they are widely used to stop rotten-egg smell and protect tanks in softened water. Corro-Protec backs its rods with a 20-year warranty. They only protect while plugged in, so they need a reliable outlet and a quick check now and then.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size anode rod do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most residential tank heaters take a 3/4-inch NPT anode about 40 to 44 inches long, removed with a 1-1/16-inch socket. Match the length of the old rod, use a shorter rod for short tanks, and avoid RV rods, which are only a few inches long. Check whether your heater has a hex port or an outlet anode first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my water heater has a combo anode?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look at the top of the tank. If there is a separate hex plug, you have a dedicated anode port. If there is no hex plug and the hot water pipe connects through a fitting on a short nipple, the anode is built into the outlet. Bradford White uses outlet anodes on most residential models.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a flexible anode rod?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Flexible segmented rods protect the tank in the same way as solid ones and are designed for heaters with little headroom. They feed in a section at a time, and Eastman lists a 12-inch clearance minimum for its outlet rod. Solid rods have no joints, so they are a good choice where you have the full clearance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a water heater anode rod be replaced?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many manufacturers suggest inspecting every two to three years and replacing when the core wire is exposed or most of the metal is gone. Softened water, hot settings and heavy use wear rods faster, so check more often in those homes. A powered anode does not need replacing but should be checked to confirm it is powered.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
